WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.021
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.021 --> 00:00:08.860
We live in a world that is filled with so
much noise.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.963
A 2018 Deloitte study said the average
American picks up their phone

00:00:14.963 --> 00:00:16.172
52 times a day.

00:00:16.172 --> 00:00:19.634
Now, you may be thinking and
doing the math, that's twice an hour,

00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:20.660
that's not so bad.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.993
But when you're factor in the time that
you're spending sleeping, eating, driving

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:31.761
a car, having sex, things you should all
be doing without a phone in your hand.

00:00:31.761 --> 00:00:35.625
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's really more [LAUGH] like once

00:00:35.625 --> 00:00:37.290
every ten minutes.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.710
When was the last time you voluntarily
turned off your phone for 24 hours?

00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:46.519
48 hours?

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:51.575
I would say that the device in
your pocket, all the alerts,

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:58.368
the dings, the bings, the emails,
is keeping you from living your best life.

00:00:58.368 --> 00:01:02.627
And I'm not talking about the Kardashian
best life, [LAUGH] I'm talking about

00:01:02.627 --> 00:01:06.576
a life that is aligned intentionally
with your values and your desires.

00:01:09.379 --> 00:01:12.914
Eight years ago,
I was not living my best life.

00:01:12.914 --> 00:01:18.545
I was living a good life,
but it was not my best life.

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:21.157
I was leading two teams of 45 people,

00:01:21.157 --> 00:01:26.880
creating 450 pieces of editorial content
each month, for 2 million Americans.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.120
You might say I was contributing
to the content problem.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.380
I was working 12 to 15 hour days trying
to manage those 45 direct reports.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.442
And every morning I would
get up at 7:00 AM and

00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:42.363
the first thing I would
do was check my phone.

00:01:42.363 --> 00:01:46.733
I would then commute up to the city for
an hour, have a full day,

00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:51.860
commute back down, have dinner,
get back on my laptop typing my emails.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.867
And every night before I went to bed,
whether it was at 1:30,

00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:01.966
2:00, 2:30, 3:00,
the last thing I did every night

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:07.720
was check my phone one last time, to see
if there was an email that I missed.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.000
That same year, on a Saturday in October,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.400
I had a Friday just like the Friday,
a day that I told you about.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.717
I remember that I was tired,
but I still wanted to make that

00:02:24.717 --> 00:02:30.265
drive to the city to get a drink
with my friend for her birthday.

00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:35.370
I remember driving to the city, I remember
it was a cold San Francisco night.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.266
Pretty much every night
in San Francisco is cold.

00:02:37.266 --> 00:02:40.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I remember huddling under the heat lamp

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.428
trying to get warm.

00:02:41.428 --> 00:02:47.080
I remember feeling tired and
then I started to feel odd.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.032
I started to feel really weird.

00:02:54.032 --> 00:02:57.407
And I remember I walked over to the bar to
get a glass of water hoping that glass of

00:02:57.407 --> 00:02:59.214
water would somehow make me feel better.

00:02:59.214 --> 00:03:03.631
And the next thing I knew I
was on the floor of the bar.

00:03:05.374 --> 00:03:06.385
I had fainted.

00:03:07.973 --> 00:03:12.073
So this is the part of the story
where most people say this was

00:03:12.073 --> 00:03:13.620
the turning point.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.010
This is the moment that I realized
my life really needed to change.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.275
That is not how this story goes.

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:22.611
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The next day we went to the doctor and

00:03:22.611 --> 00:03:27.265
she said, you're a very normal healthy
26 year old except that you were

00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:29.681
severely dehydrated and exhausted.

00:03:29.681 --> 00:03:31.410
So I went and I fixed these two things.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.483
I rehydrated myself,
I got a little bit more sleep, and

00:03:35.483 --> 00:03:37.573
I went right back to my life.

00:03:37.573 --> 00:03:43.899
My over-worked, exhausted,
tech-addicted life.

00:03:43.899 --> 00:03:46.080
Those next eight years are really intense.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.640
We nearly ran out of money twice,
that company I was telling you about.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.530
We raised more money.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:57.490
We sold that company to a much bigger
company, taking our 250-person

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.840
organization and trying to integrate
into this 250,000 person organization.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.520
I left that company.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.090
I ended a really important relationship.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.146
But that year, that first year
that I was telling you about,

00:04:12.146 --> 00:04:15.686
I began to practice that has
transformed the way I live.

00:04:15.686 --> 00:04:20.408
That year, I began a practice of
retreating from my life in order to move

00:04:20.408 --> 00:04:24.828
forward in my life, with greater
intention and greater purpose.

00:04:24.828 --> 00:04:28.029
Every three months,
right after work on Friday,

00:04:28.029 --> 00:04:33.280
I drive up into the California hills
into that beautiful golden place.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.250
I go to my favorite retreat center,
Silence Day.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.620
I get out of my car and let the beauty
in the silence begin to fill my soul.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.840
But the thing that I do is I take
out my phone, and I turn it off, for

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.760
the next 36 hours, until Sunday afternoon.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.317
This practice has grounded me
through the storms of life.

00:04:56.317 --> 00:05:02.704
It has created space for me to simply be,
space to reflect on my life.

00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:08.503
And it's given my brain 36 hours to
detach from my technology addiction,

00:05:08.503 --> 00:05:11.325
helping my brain reset, just once.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:20.526
So I want to invite you all to join me in
retreating from your life to move forward.

00:05:20.526 --> 00:05:26.731
Maybe you're feeling like I did,
desperate, overworked.

00:05:26.731 --> 00:05:31.612
Maybe for you it's being a parent,
that daily grind.

00:05:31.612 --> 00:05:34.480
Maybe it's laundry,
chores, dishes, repeat.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.579
Maybe it's the weight of figuring
out who you want to be right now and

00:05:39.579 --> 00:05:41.487
who you want to be in the future.

00:05:44.495 --> 00:05:47.107
Now, I know some of you are thinking,
Tiffany, this is crazy.

00:05:47.107 --> 00:05:49.543
I can't take off 36 hours of my life.

00:05:49.543 --> 00:05:53.207
I know the extroverts out there
are thinking, I can't be quiet for

00:05:53.207 --> 00:05:54.919
2 hours much less 36 hours.

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:57.943
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I know the parents are thinking,

00:05:57.943 --> 00:06:01.430
I cannot abandon my co-parent for
the entire weekend.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:02.530
They will kill me when I get back.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.811
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I have a challenge for

00:06:05.811 --> 00:06:08.450
you, I'm calling it
the coffee shop challenge.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:14.267
I invite you to take a small
tech detox of just two hours.

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:18.754
This weekend, this Saturday or
Sunday, go to a coffee shop and

00:06:18.754 --> 00:06:21.219
turn off your phone for two hours.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:26.918
And during that time you can do the three
things that I do whenever I go on my

00:06:26.918 --> 00:06:27.541
retreats.

00:06:27.541 --> 00:06:29.014
First, I turn off my phone.

00:06:29.014 --> 00:06:32.465
You'll have already done that.

00:06:32.465 --> 00:06:36.913
Two, I begin to reflect on the last
three months, not your whole life,

00:06:36.913 --> 00:06:38.704
just the last three months.

00:06:38.704 --> 00:06:42.456
What worked, what didn't?

00:06:42.456 --> 00:06:43.350
What really sucked?

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.110
What do you want to do differently?

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:51.336
And then lastly, I pull out the list of
things I would love to do with my life,

00:06:51.336 --> 00:06:54.946
and I choose one thing to do
in the next three months.

00:06:54.946 --> 00:06:58.407
If you don't have that list, this weekend
is a perfect time to make that list.

00:07:03.102 --> 00:07:06.244
We live in world that is filled with so
much noise.

00:07:06.244 --> 00:07:13.491
Resist the urge to consume
more information and do more.

00:07:13.491 --> 00:07:17.208
Instead, turn inwards.

00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:18.312
My senior year at Stanford,

00:07:18.312 --> 00:07:20.759
I remember reading this line that
has stuck with me to this day.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.966
Richard Foster in the Celebration of
Discipline said, the desperate need in our

00:07:26.966 --> 00:07:31.893
world today is not for more gifted or
intelligent people but for deep people.

00:07:33.277 --> 00:07:38.292
I'm going to say it again.

00:07:38.292 --> 00:07:43.168
The desperate need in our world today
is not for more gifted people, or

00:07:43.168 --> 00:07:45.990
intelligent people, but deep people.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:53.902
Friends, you are some of the most
gifted and intelligent people I know.

00:07:53.902 --> 00:07:58.609
And your life challenge will be to figure
out what is your gift to the worlds.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:06.193
But before you lead others, before you
change lives, change organizations and

00:08:06.193 --> 00:08:11.652
change the world, I want to invite you
all to begin by changing yourself.

00:08:11.652 --> 00:08:17.889
That starts by turning off your phone and
turning inwards.

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:18.701
Thank you.

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:23.349
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:23.349 --> 00:08:26.357
[MUSIC]

